/*****************************************************************************
    concatT -- the fixture for 35a: implicit concatenation through `+=`.

    SEQ 35 amended. `field += this that and the other` concatenates instead of
    copying a list. The arm sits ABOVE opPlusEQ's isLIST->copyListTo
    short-circuit and routes through appendGroup + opString, inheriting
    appendGroup's own list traversal rather than rebuilding one.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT, and it is load-bearing: an incant parse failure
    abandons the rest of the file AND EXITS 0 (CLAUDE.md testing doctrine,
    third corollary), so a truncated run would otherwise read as a short
    successful one. This fixture's FIRST draft died exactly that way.

    ⚠ THREE FINDINGS THIS FIXTURE ENCODES. Read them before changing the arm.

    1. NO PRINT SHORTCUT PARSES IN `+=` ARGUMENT POSITION. `$`, `_` and `,+`
       ALL fail to parse there (`ERROR processCode: <action> parse failed`).
       CAUSE, per Tony: **ExpressioN does not deal with shortcuts -- PrintXP
       handles those.** The right-hand side of `+=` is an ExpressioN, and the
       shortcut vocabulary lives one grammar rule away. A known design boundary,
       not an accident, and Tony's read is that it is not a big deal.
       BUT NOTE THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS FEATURE: `+=` concatenation is
       DEFAULT-SPACING-ONLY. There is no way to write the no-space form that
       `string $"t" at` uses throughout genEmit, so the omitted form is not a
       drop-in replacement for the spelled one wherever `$` is in play.
       The brief's "$ is free -- useDefaultSpace is state outside the call,
       toggled inside appendGroup, nothing to set up" is exactly right about the
       MECHANISM and says nothing about REACHABILITY. That is the round-2 lesson
       generalised: a mechanism verified in the machinery is not thereby
       reachable from the surface, and only the surface was untested.

    2. A STRING TARGET WITH CONTENT IS *REPLACED*, NOT APPENDED, and that is
       inconsistent with `+=` itself:
           pre = "start"; pre += "x";        ->  [ startx ]     APPENDS
           pre = "start"; pre += "a" "b";    ->  [ a b  ]       REPLACES
       Cause: opString does `target.text = toString()` -- assignment. The
       one-token narrowing is to drop `|| isSTRING || isTOKEN` from the arm's
       condition, leaving `!data`, which matches the brief's own sentence
       "empty is precisely no data". NOT DONE UNILATERALLY -- the condition was
       written deliberately and the alternative (pre-loading the buffer with
       the target's existing text) changes spacing semantics between the two
       paths. Tony's/Clay's ruling.

    3. CONCATENATION LEAVES A TRAILING SPACE under default spacing --
       `x += "a" "b"` yields `a b ` (note the trailing blank), which is why the
       expected lines below show two spaces before the closing bracket.

    NOT COVERED, deliberately: `x = "a" "b"` is 35b's path, not this one, and it
    currently yields `xlInSet` -- an uninitialised read. Confirmed here to sit on
    `=` and NOT on `+=`, which is what the amended brief predicted.

    ⚠ AND ONE TRAP THIS FIXTURE FELL INTO ITSELF, worth the line: row 2 was
    first written with the local named `one` and printed `[ one ]` -- the field's
    own TAG, i.e. unset (CLAIM KANT-10). The identical statement worked in
    isolation. `one` COLLIDES with an existing name, so the assignment went
    somewhere else entirely and the failure was SILENT. Renaming to `solo` fixed
    it with no other change. Same family as the `debug` scar: a name collision
    with a node that happens to carry no visible contents produces a
    plausible-looking result and nothing complains. GREP A LOCAL'S NAME BEFORE
    USING IT IN A FIXTURE, even a throwaway one.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

register(ConcatTest);
define
    runConcat code={
        empty += "a" "b" "c";
        print "1 fresh target      [" empty "]":;
        solo += "a";
        print "2 single value      [" solo "]":;
        pre = "start";
        pre += "x";
        print "3 string + scalar   [" pre "]":;
        two = "start";
        two += "a" "b";
        print "4 string + LIST     [" two "]":;
        num = 5;
        num += 1 2;
        print "5 numeric target    [" num "]":;
        };
    ;

runConcat();
print "CT SENTINEL":;
stop();
